/*
 * EXAMPLE 4 — TicketService, singular case.
 *
 * The venue starts with 12 free seats, so the run reads the count, holds three
 * of them, commits that hold, and reads the count again.  Bracketing the hold
 * with the same query is the point: numSeatsAvailable appears twice, produces a
 * SERVER_OUTPUT in each block, and the two captures must stay independent — the
 * first must not be threaded into the second.
 *
 * seatHoldId is NOT bound here.  The venue assigns it, so the caller cannot know
 * it before the call; it is captured from findAndHoldSeats and threaded into
 * reserveSeats by the propagation scan.  Binding it would be rejected by the
 * validator.
 *
 * customerEmail IS bound twice, once per block that names it, because it is a
 * CLIENT_INPUT in both.  Both bindings carry the same address on purpose:
 * reserveSeats' precondition requires the claimer to be the original holder.
 */
test TicketServiceDryRun {
    sequence: numSeatsAvailable -> findAndHoldSeats -> reserveSeats -> numSeatsAvailable;

    inputs {
        findAndHoldSeats[1].numSeats = 3;
        findAndHoldSeats[1].customerEmail = "alice@example.com";
        reserveSeats[2].customerEmail = "alice@example.com";
    }
}
